WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.007 --> 00:00:00.974
- In this episode,

00:00:00.974 --> 00:00:03.562
I talk about giving away
your best work for free,

00:00:03.562 --> 00:00:05.537
hitting the pavement,

00:00:05.537 --> 00:00:08.188
and we spoke to Darth Bill.

00:00:08.188 --> 00:00:11.678
(hip hop music)

00:00:11.678 --> 00:00:14.451
- You ask questions, and I answer them.

00:00:16.131 --> 00:00:20.185
This is The #AskGaryVee Show.

00:00:20.185 --> 00:00:24.804
(hip hop music)

00:00:26.504 --> 00:00:28.745
- Hey everybody, this is Gary Vay-ner-chuk,

00:00:28.745 --> 00:00:33.051
and this is Episode 73
of The #AskGaryVee Show.

00:00:33.051 --> 00:00:34.649
Before we get into the show,

00:00:34.649 --> 00:00:36.116
I think you guys probably could tell,

00:00:36.116 --> 00:00:39.303
I'm coming in with a very
serious attitude for Episode 73.

00:00:39.303 --> 00:00:41.287
Just came out of an intense meeting,

00:00:41.287 --> 00:00:43.456
got more more intense
meetings on the backside,

00:00:43.456 --> 00:00:47.947
and so, you should be
prepared for intense.

00:00:48.697 --> 00:00:51.013
- [Voiceover] Carlo
asks, "I sometimes feel

00:00:51.013 --> 00:00:53.195
"that giving away some
of my best work for free

00:00:53.195 --> 00:00:55.437
"might mean others perceiving
it as less valuable.

00:00:55.437 --> 00:00:56.668
"Is this a true risk,

00:00:56.668 --> 00:00:58.857
"or is this where the right
hook balances it all out?"

00:00:58.857 --> 00:01:00.403
- Carlo, this is a solid question.

00:01:00.403 --> 00:01:01.785
You know, I think at some level,

00:01:01.785 --> 00:01:05.385
there is a risk about giving
away your best work for free.

00:01:05.385 --> 00:01:08.616
For me, that's been the
gateway drug at some level,

00:01:08.616 --> 00:01:11.071
because I think best
work needs to be defined.

00:01:11.071 --> 00:01:12.487
It depends on what your work is.

00:01:12.487 --> 00:01:14.471
For example, I'm blown away by people

00:01:14.471 --> 00:01:16.457
who don't realize that their best advice

00:01:16.457 --> 00:01:18.484
is exactly what you
should give away for free,

00:01:18.484 --> 00:01:21.088
because if you're in the
selling-advice business,

00:01:21.088 --> 00:01:23.400
you really need to give contextual advice.

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:25.816
So I can theoretically talk
about Snapchat being important,

00:01:25.816 --> 00:01:27.460
but then when I meet the tire company,

00:01:27.460 --> 00:01:30.436
we have to formulate it to
work within that context,

00:01:30.436 --> 00:01:33.038
and so that advice is
then gonna be specific.

00:01:33.038 --> 00:01:34.410
If you're a painter,

00:01:34.410 --> 00:01:37.571
and your best work is the
greatest painting you ever made,

00:01:37.571 --> 00:01:38.854
and then you gave it away for free,

00:01:38.854 --> 00:01:40.334
that's maybe a little
bit of a different game.

00:01:40.334 --> 00:01:42.045
But if you strategically gave it away,

00:01:42.045 --> 00:01:44.142
like to a very important museum,

00:01:44.142 --> 00:01:46.487
or to a very important billionaire,

00:01:46.487 --> 00:01:49.220
that puts it in the
prime spot in their home,

00:01:49.220 --> 00:01:51.108
could that then become the gateway?

00:01:51.108 --> 00:01:52.390
The problem is,

00:01:53.070 --> 00:01:55.510
with this question and this debate,

00:01:55.510 --> 00:01:58.688
is that when you are
doing something for free,

00:01:58.688 --> 00:02:00.777
it needs to be strategic,

00:02:00.777 --> 00:02:02.147
because what you're looking to do

00:02:02.147 --> 00:02:04.764
by giving something for
free, is to create leverage,

00:02:04.764 --> 00:02:07.242
to then do something that is not for free.

00:02:07.242 --> 00:02:09.752
And so, you know, DRock
could've made a video

00:02:09.752 --> 00:02:11.321
for a bunch of different characters,

00:02:11.321 --> 00:02:14.496
and maybe it wouldn't have
panned out to have the ROI

00:02:14.496 --> 00:02:18.224
that he received by doing
a video for me for free.

00:02:18.224 --> 00:02:21.429
And so, I think that often times,

00:02:21.429 --> 00:02:23.541
people look at this as
a blanket statement.

00:02:23.541 --> 00:02:26.564
To me, all the good stuff
I give away for free

00:02:26.564 --> 00:02:28.398
has strategic purpose.

00:02:28.398 --> 00:02:30.769
I understand why I'm doing it,

00:02:30.769 --> 00:02:34.269
and that's why it becomes
so much easier to do,

00:02:34.269 --> 00:02:37.515
even though I don't always expect

00:02:37.515 --> 00:02:39.605
the outcome of that action.

00:02:39.605 --> 00:02:41.140
And I think that becomes the big part.

00:02:41.140 --> 00:02:43.377
Way too many people do things for free,

00:02:43.377 --> 00:02:44.830
give away their best work for free,

00:02:44.830 --> 00:02:47.540
and then expect this windfall behind it,

00:02:47.540 --> 00:02:50.697
and when it's not delivered,
they become disappointed,

00:02:50.697 --> 00:02:52.126
and within that disappointment,

00:02:52.126 --> 00:02:54.174
they don't follow up, and do it again,

00:02:54.174 --> 00:02:56.108
and make it a replicable action,

00:02:56.108 --> 00:02:59.125
which has more upside in
three out of five times,

00:02:59.125 --> 00:03:00.124
where it brings value.

00:03:00.124 --> 00:03:01.794
You focused on the two out of five,

00:03:01.794 --> 00:03:03.730
and that kinda squashed you, so

00:03:04.410 --> 00:03:05.436
that's my answer.

00:03:05.886 --> 00:03:08.733
- [Voiceover] Darth Bill asks,
"Should a new small business

00:03:08.733 --> 00:03:10.251
"join a local Chamber of Commerce

00:03:10.251 --> 00:03:12.467
"in order to build a brand in
the community they live in?

00:03:12.467 --> 00:03:15.130
"I've never had much ROI
on networking groups."

00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:18.176
- You know, Darth Bill,
the answer is probably,

00:03:18.176 --> 00:03:20.481
but maybe it's not the group's problem,

00:03:20.481 --> 00:03:21.586
maybe it's your problem.

00:03:21.586 --> 00:03:23.448
Maybe you're not good
at networking, right?

00:03:23.448 --> 00:03:24.898
And so, you know,

00:03:24.898 --> 00:03:27.132
I think you need to look
yourself in the mirror, Darth.

00:03:29.652 --> 00:03:31.119
And I'm gonna continue with the pause,

00:03:31.119 --> 00:03:33.206
cuz I don't wanna leave it
that negative, cuz I like Bill.

00:03:33.206 --> 00:03:34.804
Even though I'm in a little
bit of a focused mood,

00:03:34.804 --> 00:03:37.325
there's no reason to be zingy.

00:03:37.325 --> 00:03:39.313
You know, I think you need
to really know yourself.

00:03:39.313 --> 00:03:40.344
One of the biggest reasons

00:03:40.344 --> 00:03:41.952
I do go to a lot of networking things,

00:03:41.952 --> 00:03:43.343
and I don't go to Chamber of Commerce,

00:03:43.343 --> 00:03:45.203
though I did, we were part of one.

00:03:45.203 --> 00:03:46.245
Brandon loved it.

00:03:46.245 --> 00:03:47.767
I (mumbles) freaking loved the concept

00:03:47.767 --> 00:03:48.829
of the Chamber of Commerce.

00:03:48.829 --> 00:03:50.715
I mean, you know, I think

00:03:50.715 --> 00:03:52.116
that you really need to know yourself,

00:03:52.116 --> 00:03:53.638
and if you don't find yourself

00:03:53.638 --> 00:03:56.189
as someone who is strong at networking,

00:03:56.189 --> 00:03:59.523
or has that gear to go up
to people and say hello,

00:03:59.523 --> 00:04:01.405
and you don't have that bullcrap gear,

00:04:01.405 --> 00:04:02.629
where you're just going up to everybody

00:04:02.629 --> 00:04:03.923
and handing your business card,

00:04:04.713 --> 00:04:07.276
you know, then maybe it's not for you.

00:04:07.276 --> 00:04:09.064
I mean, we really need
to bet on strengths.

00:04:09.064 --> 00:04:10.344
You know that's a big thesis of mine.

00:04:10.344 --> 00:04:12.199
And so I would look at yourself first.

00:04:12.199 --> 00:04:14.112
If you're not good in that environment,

00:04:14.112 --> 00:04:15.655
then maybe you shouldn't
be putting yourself

00:04:15.655 --> 00:04:16.459
in that position.

00:04:17.260 --> 00:04:18.843
- [Voiceover] Heromoviepodcast asks,

00:04:18.843 --> 00:04:20.425
"What value do you place

00:04:20.425 --> 00:04:22.137
"on getting out there
and pressing the flesh,

00:04:22.137 --> 00:04:23.756
"in order to expand your fanbase?"

00:04:24.326 --> 00:04:25.837
- Heromoviepodcast,

00:04:27.977 --> 00:04:29.869
can I call you HMP?

00:04:29.869 --> 00:04:30.620
I think I can.

00:04:30.620 --> 00:04:34.469
HMP, you know, I have
enormous value in it.

00:04:34.469 --> 00:04:35.833
You go into a confined area,

00:04:35.833 --> 00:04:39.063
where there's like-minded
fans to exactly what you do.

00:04:39.063 --> 00:04:40.977
And, you know, listen,

00:04:40.977 --> 00:04:42.907
nothing replaces a nice little selfie,

00:04:42.907 --> 00:04:44.550
or a hug, or a high-five.

00:04:44.550 --> 00:04:46.195
You know, I think you use

00:04:46.195 --> 00:04:47.907
the digital world as a gateway drug,

00:04:47.907 --> 00:04:50.375
to then meet in person, to then solidify,

00:04:50.375 --> 00:04:52.067
and then kick back out
into the digital world

00:04:52.067 --> 00:04:53.469
to create amplification.

00:04:53.469 --> 00:04:56.461
So, I'm a huge fan of
pounding the pavement,

00:04:56.461 --> 00:04:58.985
showing up in real world places,

00:04:58.985 --> 00:05:00.800
and it continues to be a reason

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:02.541
I go to things like South by Southwest,

00:05:02.541 --> 00:05:04.087
and I'm a big fan of it.

00:05:04.087 --> 00:05:06.972
I don't think you can just
do it in the data world,

00:05:06.972 --> 00:05:08.794
we're still human, for now.

00:05:08.794 --> 00:05:11.418
Robot culture coming, but
not right this second,

00:05:11.418 --> 00:05:12.904
and so market in the
year that you live in.

00:05:12.904 --> 00:05:14.651
2015, hitting the pavement?

00:05:14.651 --> 00:05:15.282
Yes.

00:05:16.052 --> 00:05:17.843
- Hey Gary, my name is Pavel Kravchuk.

00:05:17.843 --> 00:05:19.593
I'm here with members of Slavic Chorale.

00:05:19.593 --> 00:05:20.712
- [Both] Hi, Gary.

00:05:21.252 --> 00:05:23.809
- We just sang at
VaynerMedia San Francisco,

00:05:23.809 --> 00:05:25.998
and we have a question for you.

00:05:25.998 --> 00:05:27.014
Answer our question.

00:05:28.194 --> 00:05:29.809
We are a choir and orchestra

00:05:29.809 --> 00:05:32.148
that has existed for about five years,

00:05:32.148 --> 00:05:34.204
and we're looking to grow.

00:05:34.204 --> 00:05:38.523
We have an audience of about 3
to 5,000 at our big concerts.

00:05:38.523 --> 00:05:40.875
In order for us to continue to grow,

00:05:40.875 --> 00:05:43.306
we need to get more corporate sponsorships

00:05:43.306 --> 00:05:44.556
from bigger companies,

00:05:44.556 --> 00:05:47.122
but they already support orchestras

00:05:47.122 --> 00:05:48.685
like the San Francisco Symphony.

00:05:48.685 --> 00:05:51.413
So how do we get them
to support us as well?

00:05:51.413 --> 00:05:54.657
- First of all, thank you so
much for coming to Vayner SF,

00:05:54.657 --> 00:05:57.062
and wowing them with your skills.

00:05:57.062 --> 00:06:00.075
You know, look, I made a
very classic, old-school

00:06:00.075 --> 00:06:02.704
Gary Vaynerchuk video from way back when.

00:06:02.704 --> 00:06:04.245
I'll link it up right here, DRock.

00:06:04.245 --> 00:06:06.786
You know, this one is the cold call video.

00:06:07.526 --> 00:06:10.661
This seems to be a very
simple answer, my friends.

00:06:10.661 --> 00:06:13.735
I think that you guys may
be tremendous at singing,

00:06:13.735 --> 00:06:15.956
but selling might be a
very different thing.

00:06:15.956 --> 00:06:18.189
And so you need to find
who can sell and sing,

00:06:18.189 --> 00:06:20.016
or who can just sell, while you guys sing,

00:06:20.016 --> 00:06:23.769
because the truth is, you
just hit the ground, right?

00:06:23.769 --> 00:06:26.154
You like, there's a billion places to go,

00:06:26.154 --> 00:06:28.446
and you just literally go ask all of them.

00:06:28.446 --> 00:06:30.831
I'm always blown away by people saying,

00:06:30.831 --> 00:06:32.061
"Oh, we're in big trouble."

00:06:32.061 --> 00:06:34.224
Like, people are supporting other things,

00:06:34.224 --> 00:06:35.040
or there's nowhere to go.

00:06:35.040 --> 00:06:36.426
There's always somewhere to go.

00:06:36.426 --> 00:06:37.200
Get creative.

00:06:37.200 --> 00:06:40.578
Like, you know, are they in San Francisco?

00:06:40.578 --> 00:06:44.420
Yeah, I mean, Jesus Christ,
you've got nothing but funded,

00:06:44.420 --> 00:06:47.088
read TechCrunch and Re/code everyday,

00:06:47.088 --> 00:06:48.454
look at every code company

00:06:48.454 --> 00:06:50.427
that just raised 50 million dollars,

00:06:50.427 --> 00:06:53.011
and go knock on their door
and say, "Give me 3,000."

00:06:53.011 --> 00:06:56.037
I mean, like, you know, I
mean it's like, it's crazy.

00:06:56.827 --> 00:06:59.583
You know, this question to a salesman

00:06:59.583 --> 00:07:02.439
is like me asking you, "How do I sing?"

00:07:02.439 --> 00:07:03.049
Right?

00:07:03.049 --> 00:07:05.600
Like, you would answer, "You just sing."

00:07:05.600 --> 00:07:06.961
Now, what the funny part is,

00:07:06.961 --> 00:07:09.671
is I just might suck at singing.

00:07:09.671 --> 00:07:10.228
Right?

00:07:10.228 --> 00:07:11.540
And you might suck at sales,

00:07:11.540 --> 00:07:12.959
and so I think you need to figure out

00:07:12.959 --> 00:07:14.245
who can do that for you,

00:07:14.245 --> 00:07:16.365
because the answer to your
question is extremely basic.

00:07:16.365 --> 00:07:19.298
Literally go to the phone book.

00:07:19.298 --> 00:07:20.499
What I mean by that is,

00:07:20.499 --> 00:07:23.941
literally hit up every single
business in San Francisco.

00:07:23.941 --> 00:07:24.748
All of them.

00:07:24.748 --> 00:07:27.464
Rick's Car Wash to Google.

00:07:27.464 --> 00:07:31.203
From everybody from Rick's to Google.

00:07:31.203 --> 00:07:33.909
That's right, DRock, you
weren't expecting that, were you?

00:07:33.909 --> 00:07:36.845
And so, Google, Rick's Car Wash,

00:07:36.845 --> 00:07:39.306
and every company in between
gets hit up by you guys.

00:07:39.306 --> 00:07:41.071
Somebody's gonna give you cash,

00:07:41.071 --> 00:07:43.462
and I'm gonna guess it's gonna
be more than just somebody.

00:07:44.022 --> 00:07:46.806
- [Voiceover] Apollopoetry asks,
"When all is said and done,

00:07:46.806 --> 00:07:48.242
"how would you like to be remembered?"

00:07:49.392 --> 00:07:51.450
- This is a tremendous question.

00:07:51.450 --> 00:07:53.705
This finally put me in a decent mood.

00:07:53.705 --> 00:07:54.488
You know, look.

00:07:54.488 --> 00:07:57.533
I think, I think that selfishly,

00:07:57.533 --> 00:07:59.474
I would like to be remembered as

00:07:59.474 --> 00:08:02.488
the greatest human being
that ever lived the Earth.

00:08:02.488 --> 00:08:03.308
(bell rings)

00:08:03.308 --> 00:08:06.308
You know, we'll try to
keep it basic and modest.

00:08:06.658 --> 00:08:08.999
You know, I'd like to think that,

00:08:09.439 --> 00:08:13.065
I'd like to think that
people appreciated my effort,

00:08:14.705 --> 00:08:17.486
in whatever way they define that, right?

00:08:17.486 --> 00:08:20.057
Like, the hustle culture
that I've created,

00:08:20.057 --> 00:08:21.311
that you know, is clearly,

00:08:21.931 --> 00:08:23.399
I'm so humbled by the notion

00:08:23.399 --> 00:08:26.115
that it's inspired people to
work harder and create things,

00:08:26.115 --> 00:08:28.441
but like, the effort on
like, on an individual level,

00:08:28.441 --> 00:08:30.384
and like, all the behind the scenes stuff.

00:08:30.384 --> 00:08:31.752
Like, look, I often say,

00:08:31.752 --> 00:08:33.624
I live my life

00:08:34.674 --> 00:08:37.394
on a daily basis, with the complete intent

00:08:37.394 --> 00:08:39.523
to have everybody that I interact with,

00:08:39.523 --> 00:08:40.852
show up to my funeral.

00:08:40.852 --> 00:08:42.804
And I think the only way you can do that,

00:08:42.804 --> 00:08:45.291
is to be remembered as such a good person,

00:08:45.291 --> 00:08:46.741
that you created guilt,

00:08:46.741 --> 00:08:48.278
that people have to go out of their way

00:08:48.278 --> 00:08:50.304
on their busy days to
show up to your funeral,

00:08:50.304 --> 00:08:52.300
even though you only met twice
in two business meetings.

00:08:52.300 --> 00:08:53.501
And so how do you do that?

00:08:53.501 --> 00:08:54.453
You become memorable.

00:08:54.453 --> 00:08:58.314
I take advantage of like,
my extroverted personality,

00:08:58.314 --> 00:09:00.168
and like, the charisma that I was gifted,

00:09:01.098 --> 00:09:03.469
but I think you need to
back up that sizzle, right?

00:09:03.469 --> 00:09:05.025
And I take a lot of pride in my

00:09:05.025 --> 00:09:08.670
ability to be a practitioner,
and my ability to do,

00:09:08.670 --> 00:09:10.795
I've gotten better and better and better

00:09:10.795 --> 00:09:12.176
at like, keeping my word.

00:09:12.176 --> 00:09:13.897
That's always, you know, tough,

00:09:13.897 --> 00:09:16.325
because, you know, I would
have all these ambitions,

00:09:16.325 --> 00:09:17.937
and I'm always on the offense,

00:09:17.937 --> 00:09:20.094
and that is something
I've gotten better at.

00:09:20.884 --> 00:09:22.007
You know, so I guess, you know,

00:09:22.007 --> 00:09:25.179
just a good human being who cared about,

00:09:26.109 --> 00:09:28.374
who cared about more
things than just himself.

00:09:29.204 --> 00:09:30.209
Question of the day.

00:09:32.169 --> 00:09:33.695
What do I wanna know today?

00:09:33.695 --> 00:09:37.255
I'm in a interesting zone
today, like very focused.

00:09:37.255 --> 00:09:38.386
Usually when the lights come on,

00:09:38.386 --> 00:09:39.816
I can get out of it,
and go into this zone,

00:09:39.816 --> 00:09:42.334
but I'm in hardcore focus mode.

00:09:42.334 --> 00:09:45.156
Talk to me about what you are focusing on

00:09:45.156 --> 00:09:49.157
the most in your life right now.

00:09:49.157 --> 00:09:52.373
Thanks for watching Episode 73.

00:09:52.373 --> 00:09:53.849
Getting close to 100, guys.

